## 1. Introduction -

Have you ever wondered how a busy modern library handles tens of thousands of books, tracks hundreds of active loans, calculates overdue fines down to the penny, and handles instant reservations without devolving into complete operational chaos? The secret lies in a carefully designed Relational Database Management System (RDBMS) that turns complex physical workflows into seamless, automated database operations.
After completing this lesson, you will be able to:
Analyze the relational architecture of a 15-table library management schema connected via primary and foreign keys.
Evaluate functional and non-functional system requirements ranging from circulation management to data integrity and scalability.
Implement advanced SQL queries, aggregate operations, subqueries, and set operations to extract meaningful insights.
Construct automated database mechanisms using SQL views, stored procedures, and dynamic triggers.
Blueprinting the Schema: 15 Core Tables and Their Relationships
At the heart of an efficient library system is a relational database designed to reduce manual work and maintain strict data accuracy. Rather than storing information in a giant, unwieldy spreadsheet, the database divides library entities into 15 normalized tables, each with a distinct purpose and linked via Primary Keys (PK) and Foreign Keys (FK).
To understand how the data flows, imagine building the database in three interconnected functional layers:
User & Staff Entities:
Student: Stores member profiles including Member_ID (PK), Name, Email, Phone_No, Department, and academic Year.
Librarian: Stores administrative user accounts with Librarian_ID (PK), Username, Password for authentication, and contact details.
Catalog & Location Entities:
Book: Functions as the master title catalog containing Book_ID (PK), Title, ISBN, Price, and Quantity. It links via foreign keys to Author (Author_ID), Publisher (Publisher_ID), and Category (Category_ID).
Book_Copy: Tracks individual physical copies using Copy_ID (PK), linking back to Book_ID, Library_ID, and Shelf_ID. It maintains individual physical copy Status (available, issued, damaged, or lost) and Purchase_Date.
Shelf & Library: Manage physical placement, tracking maximum capacity and locations across library branches.
Circulation & Transaction Entities:
Issue_Borrow (created as Issue in MySQL): Records borrowing activities connecting Member_ID, Book_ID, and Librarian_ID with Issue_Date and Due_Date.
Return_Book (created as Return in MySQL): Logs returned items, Return_Date, and calculates any late charges under Fine_Amount.
Fine & Fine_Payment: Track individual member charges, payment methods (e.g., cash, card), and transaction status (paid or pending).
Reservation & Book_Review: Allow students to queue for checked-out books and submit book feedback and star ratings.
